          "abstract": "<jats:p>Process data reconciliation and gross-error detection are usually formulated as estimation and residual-screening problems. In plant operation, the decision problem is wider: incompatible data may arise from a biased instrument, an invalid balance, an unmodelled topology or inventory term, a wrong operating section, or a time interval that should not be treated as steady. This paper proposes a repair-context formulation of Bayesian data reconciliation in which these alternatives are compared within one executable Bayesian catalogue. The catalogue assigns priors to measurement-bias supports, relation slack variables, topology or operating-context changes, and time-cover partitions. Measurements define measurement contexts, balances and model equations define relation contexts, operating modes define operating contexts, and historian intervals define time-cover contexts. Reconciliation constructs a coherent state for an accepted context diagram; gross-error detection becomes sparse repair of measurement contexts; relation slack repairs balance or topology contexts; time-window splitting repairs a cover; and operating-mode change replaces the section through which historian data are interpreted. The framework is tested on two benchmark families. The P15 steam-metering benchmark is parsed from Scilab source, giving 28 measured stream contexts and 11 relation contexts. The Tennessee Eastman benchmark is executed from a materialized Git-LFS archive containing 214 Excel workbooks. P15 tests algebraic reconciliation, sparse gross-error detection, relation/topology ambiguity, and steady-window repair. Tennessee Eastman tests historian-scale behavior under an injected sensor error, standard faults 6 and 14, and Mode 3 under a Mode 1 cover. The contribution is a local-to-global diagnostic layer with posterior repair-context probabilities.</jats:p>",
          "type": "posted-content",
          "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7035466"
        },
